TITLE:: FxLfo
summary:: one low frequency oscillator, seven shapes
categories:: Libraries>FxKit
related:: Classes/FxParamUnit

DESCRIPTION::
Sine, triangle, saw up, saw down, square, sample and hold, noise, chosen by a control.

code::
{ SinOsc.ar(440 * (2 ** FxLfo.kr(1, 2))) * 0.1 ! 2 }.play;
::

subsection:: On a cycle clock
With a fourth argument, the index of one of link::#*syncNames::, the lfo can run
on a clock instead of its rate: one period every so many cycles. The clock is
three controls that every effect in one graph shares: code::fxCps:: (cycles a
second, 0.5625 unless told), code::fxCycle:: (where the clock was when told) and
code::fxSync:: (a trigger sent with fxCycle). From the trigger the position runs
on at fxCps, so a clock told now and then keeps the phase. SuperDirtGUI tells it
from Tidal's events (SDGFxControl). Without the fourth argument the graph has no
clock controls and nothing changes.

code::
x = { SinOsc.ar(440 * (2 ** FxLfo.kr(4, 1, 0, \sync.kr(5)))) * 0.1 ! 2 }.play;  // a square on 1/4 cycle
x.set(\fxCps, 0.5, \fxCycle, 0, \fxSync, 1);   // 0.5 cycles a second, the cycle starts now
x.free;
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: shapes
Their names.

METHOD:: kr
-1..1.
ARGUMENT:: shape
ARGUMENT:: rate
ARGUMENT:: phase
ARGUMENT:: sync
an index into link::#*syncNames::, a number or a control; 0 is free. Leave it out for no clock.

METHOD:: syncNames
code::free::, then a period in cycles, as words so a Tidal pattern can say them: four, two, one, half, quarter, eighth, sixteenth, third, sixth, twelfth (4 to 1/12 of a cycle). For a menu in a table.

METHOD:: syncCycles
The periods in cycles, by the same index.

METHOD:: clock
Inside a SynthDef function: code::[cps, position]::, the clock's speed and where it is in cycles.

METHOD:: at
The same in numbers, for a display. The two random ones draw as 0.
ARGUMENT:: shape
ARGUMENT:: ph

INSTANCEMETHODS::

